---
import Architecture from '../../layouts/Architecture.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/arch/Diagram.astro';
import Box from '../../components/arch/Box.astro';
import Edge from '../../components/arch/Edge.astro';
import Group from '../../components/arch/Group.astro';
import Label from '../../components/arch/Label.astro';
import Legend from '../../components/arch/Legend.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/arch/Callout.astro';
---
<Architecture
  title="Overview"
  subtitle="Every moving piece of pixi-reels on one page, and how they are wired."
  eyebrow="Start here"
>
  <p>
    A slot machine looks simple from the outside: press a button, five columns
    blur, five columns stop, you see a grid. Inside a real game that moment is
    a surprising amount of coordination. Something has to fetch the result from
    a server while the reels are already moving, hand each column the exact
    symbols it must land on, stop them one after another with a satisfying
    bounce, let one column hesitate for drama, and survive a player who taps
    the button twenty times a second. pixi-reels is that machinery, packaged so
    you can build the game around it instead of building it again.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="plain">
    You describe the board once with a builder. You get back one PixiJS
    container, the <code>ReelSet</code>, that you add to your stage. From then
    on you talk to it in three verbs: <code>spin()</code>, <code>setResult()</code> when the server answers, and <code>events.on()</code> to hear what happened.
    Everything else on this page is what happens between those calls.
  </Callout>

  <h2>Three rings</h2>

  <p>
    It helps to picture the library as three rings around your game. The
    innermost ring is the <strong>builder</strong>: a fluent object that
    collects your configuration and, in one <code>build()</code> call, checks
    it and constructs everything. The middle ring is the <strong>reel set</strong>: the container that owns the reels, the spin
    controller, the speed profiles and the win spotlight. The outer ring is
    optional tooling, <strong>testing</strong> and <strong>debug</strong>,
    that attaches from the outside and never ships in your bundle unless you
    import it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Figure 1 is the middle ring after <code>build()</code> returns. The boxes
    inside the ReelSet are its subsystems; each one has its own page in this
    tour.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={430} caption="What you construct, and what build() hands back. The ReelSet owns six subsystems and one display child, the viewport.">
    <Box x={20} y={20} w={200} h={60} title="Your game" kind="ghost" sub="spin(), setResult(), events" mono={false} />
    <Box x={20} y={120} w={200} h={78} title="ReelSetBuilder" kind="primary" sub={['.reels(5).visibleCells(3)', '.symbols(...).build()']} />
    <Box x={20} y={240} w={200} h={60} title="PIXI.Ticker" kind="code" sub="app.ticker, via TickerRef" />
    <Box x={20} y={330} w={200} h={60} title="gsap" kind="code" sub="per set, via .gsap(instance)" />

    <Edge from={[120, 80]} to={[120, 120]} />
    <Edge from={[220, 159]} to={[260, 159]} label="build()" kind="primary" labelDy={-52} />
    <Edge from={[220, 270]} to={[260, 270]} />
    <Edge from={[220, 360]} to={[260, 360]} />

    <Group x={260} y={20} w={440} h={390} kind="tint" label="ReelSet" note="a PIXI.Container" />
    <Box x={276} y={60} w={200} h={78} title="SpinController" kind="accent" sub={['runs the phases', 'per reel']} />
    <Box x={488} y={60} w={196} h={78} title="SpeedManager" sub={['normal, turbo,', 'super turbo']} />
    <Box x={276} y={154} w={200} h={78} title="FrameBuilder" sub={['which symbols', 'land where']} />
    <Box x={488} y={154} w={196} h={78} title="SymbolFactory" sub={['one ObjectPool', 'per symbol id']} />
    <Box x={276} y={248} w={200} h={78} title="SymbolSpotlight" sub={['dim losers,', 'promote winners']} />
    <Box x={488} y={248} w={196} h={78} title="EventEmitter" sub={['59 typed events', 'spin:*, skip:*, ...']} />
    <Box x={276} y={342} w={408} h={52} title="ReelViewport" kind="subtle" sub="the only display child: five reels under one mask" />
  </Diagram>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>SpinController</strong> is the conductor. It walks each reel through its phases (start, spin, maybe anticipation, stop), holds every reel back until your result has arrived, and turns a skip press into a slam or a quicken. It never draws and never chooses an outcome.</li>
    <li><strong>SpeedManager</strong> holds named timing profiles, normal, turbo and super turbo out of the box, and lets you tune any phase at runtime.</li>
    <li><strong>FrameBuilder</strong> turns your result grid into the exact list of symbols each reel will show, buffers included, before the reel starts stopping.</li>
    <li><strong>SymbolFactory</strong> hands out symbol instances from a pool, one pool per symbol id, so a five-second spin allocates nothing.</li>
    <li><strong>SymbolSpotlight</strong> is the win presentation primitive: dim the losers, lift the winners above the mask, play their animation.</li>
    <li><strong>EventEmitter</strong> is how the set talks back. Fifty-nine typed events, from <code>spin:start</code> to <code>pin:expired</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What a reel is</h2>

  <p>
    The word "reel" usually means a column on screen. In pixi-reels a reel is
    a <em>strip</em>: an array of symbols that is longer than the window you
    see. The extra symbols sit just outside the visible area, above and below
    it, so there is always something ready to scroll in. The window itself is
    a mask on the viewport. Figure 2 shows the five strips under that mask.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={330} caption="Inside the viewport. Each Reel is a strip longer than the window; the mask hides the buffer cells above and below it.">
    <Group x={20} y={20} w={680} h={290} kind="dashed" label="ReelViewport" note="maskedContainer" />
    {[0, 1, 2, 3, 4].map((i) => (
      <Box
        x={40 + i * 130}
        y={62}
        w={120}
        h={150}
        title={`Reel ${i}`}
        kind="code"
        sub={['ReelAxis', 'ReelMotion', 'StopSequencer', 'ReelSymbol[]']}
      />
    ))}
    <rect x={30} y={100} width={660} height={74} rx="2" class="guide-strong" style="stroke-dasharray: 6 4" />
    <Label x={360} y={244} text="dashed = the visible window (the mask)" anchor="middle" tone="fg" />
    <Label x={360} y={268} text="the symbols above and below it are buffer cells, drawn but clipped" anchor="middle" />
  </Diagram>
  <Callout kind="why">
    Keeping the off-screen symbols as real, positioned objects is what makes
    everything else cheap. Scrolling is just moving the strip. Landing is just
    deciding which symbol goes into which slot as it scrolls past the edge.
    Nothing is created or destroyed while a reel moves.
  </Callout>

  <p>
    Each strip carries three small helpers. <code>ReelAxis</code> knows which
    screen axis the strip travels on and in which direction, so the same code
    runs a vertical, horizontal or reversed reel. <code>ReelMotion</code> owns
    the position of every symbol and derives it from the array index on every
    frame, which is why reels never drift. <code>StopSequencer</code> is the
    short queue of symbols the reel must land on, handed out one at a time as
    symbols wrap around. The <a href="/architecture/motion/">motion page</a> walks through one frame of all three.
  </p>

  <h2>Things that attach from outside</h2>

  <p>
    Two subpaths exist so you can look inside without shipping the tools. <code>pixi-reels/testing</code> swaps exactly two things, the Pixi ticker
    and the symbol art, and leaves everything else real, which is why a whole
    spin can run in Node in a few milliseconds. <code>pixi-reels/debug</code> draws what the engine knows (cells, buffers,
    axes, a phase timeline) onto the canvas, and <code>enableDebug()</code> in
    the main entry exposes the same facts as JSON for anyone, human or agent,
    who cannot see a canvas.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={300} caption="Two subpaths attach from outside and never ship unless imported. Testing swaps the ticker and the art; debug only reads.">
    <Box x={250} y={110} w={220} h={78} title="ReelSet" kind="primary" sub={['the thing on', 'your stage']} />
    <Group x={20} y={20} w={190} h={260} kind="solid" label="pixi-reels/testing" />
    <Box x={34} y={60} w={162} h={52} title="FakeTicker" sub="tick(ms) by hand" />
    <Box x={34} y={126} w={162} h={52} title="HeadlessSymbol" sub="draws nothing" />
    <Box x={34} y={192} w={162} h={66} title="createTestReelSet" size={13} sub={['spinAndLand,', 'expectGrid']} />
    <Group x={510} y={20} w={190} h={260} kind="solid" label="pixi-reels/debug" />
    <Box x={524} y={60} w={162} h={52} title="debugOverlay" sub="12 drawn layers" />
    <Box x={524} y={126} w={162} h={52} title="SpinMetrics" sub="rounds and phases" />
    <Box x={524} y={192} w={162} h={66} title="enableDebug" sub={['main entry:', 'JSON snapshots']} />
    <Edge from={[210, 149]} to={[250, 149]} style="dashed" />
    <Edge from={[510, 149]} to={[470, 149]} style="dashed" />
  </Diagram>
  <div class="not-prose my-8">
    <Legend items={[
      { kind: 'primary', label: 'Entry point', note: 'the one class you construct yourself' },
      { kind: 'accent', label: 'Conductor', note: 'SpinController drives every reel' },
      { kind: 'subtle', label: 'Subsystems', note: 'owned by the ReelSet, destroyed with it' },
      { kind: 'muted', label: 'Optional subpaths', note: 'testing and debug attach from outside' },
    ]} />
  </div>

  <h2>One spin, end to end</h2>

  <p>
    Here is the story the rest of this tour tells in detail. You call <code>spin()</code>. Each reel starts a little after the previous one, so
    the kickoff looks staggered. Meanwhile your code asks the server for the
    outcome and calls <code>setResult()</code> with the grid. The controller
    waits until every reel is actually spinning and until each has spun for a
    minimum time, then lets them stop one by one. As a stopping reel scrolls,
    the symbols it must land on are swapped in at the top edge, one per wrap,
    until the last one is in place; the reel snaps to the grid and bounces.
    When the last reel has bounced, the promise from <code>spin()</code> resolves with the grid and the set emits <code>spin:complete</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    If the player presses skip, the controller either slams (every reel lands
    on its result this frame) or quickens (the delays are cut, the landing
    still plays), depending on what you asked for. If a reel was set up to
    tease, it slows down and holds before it stops, and you can protect that
    tease from the first skip press. All of that is covered on the <a href="/architecture/spin-lifecycle/">spin lifecycle</a>, <a href="/architecture/timeline/">timeline</a>, <a href="/architecture/skipping/">skipping</a> and <a href="/architecture/anticipation/">anticipation</a> pages.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the code lives</h2>

  <p>
    You rarely need to open these files, but it helps to know the map when a
    stack trace points into the library.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-6 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card p-4">
      <div class="text-[11px] font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-brand-text mb-1">packages/pixi-reels/src/</div>
      <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 text-foreground/80">
        <li><code>core/</code> the set, the builder, one reel and its motion, axis, curve and viewport</li>
        <li><code>spin/</code> the controller, the phases and their steps, the spinning modes</li>
        <li><code>symbols/</code> the symbol base class and its sprite, animated, Spine and card flavours, plus the registry and factory</li>
        <li><code>frame/</code> how a result grid becomes a strip of symbols</li>
        <li><code>config/</code> types, defaults, the speed presets</li>
        <li><code>speed/</code>, <code>spotlight/</code>, <code>wins/</code> timing profiles and win presentation</li>
        <li><code>cascade/</code>, <code>board/</code>, <code>pins/</code> tumble physics, boards and Hold &amp; Win, sticky cells</li>
        <li><code>events/</code>, <code>utils/</code> the typed emitter, the ticker wrapper, gsap plumbing, notices</li>
        <li><code>debug/</code>, <code>testing/</code> the two subpaths</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card p-4">
      <div class="text-[11px] font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-brand-text mb-1">apps/site/src/runtime/</div>
      <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 text-foreground/80">
        <li><code>BlurSpriteSymbol.ts</code> a sprite symbol that swaps to a motion-blur texture while spinning</li>
        <li><code>CoinSymbol.ts</code> a coin drawn with Graphics, for Hold &amp; Win demos</li>
        <li><code>holdAndWinFx.ts</code> coin waves and collect flights</li>
        <li><code>prototypeSpriteLoader.ts</code> the open-licensed symbol atlas the demos use</li>
        <li><code>spineSets.ts</code> the bundled Spine sets behind one interface</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="text-[11px] font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-brand-text mt-4 mb-1">packages/cheats/src/</div>
      <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 text-foreground/80">
        <li><code>cheats.ts</code> forced results for demos and tests</li>
        <li><code>seededRng.ts</code> a tiny seeded random number generator</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    The recipe runtime and the cheats package are reference code, not library
    API. They live outside the package on purpose so you copy what you need
    into your own game and own it.
  </p>

  <h2>Which way the imports point</h2>

  <p>
    One rule keeps the library easy to reason about and lets the testing
    subpath be dropped from your bundle: imports only ever point down the
    stack in figure 4. The types and the event emitter know nothing about
    reels; reels know nothing about the controller; the controller knows
    nothing about the set that owns it.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={470} caption="Imports only point downward. No cycles, which is what keeps the testing subpath tree-shakeable.">
    {[
      ['config, events, utils', 'types, DEFAULTS, EventEmitter, TickerRef, gsap'],
      ['pool, symbols, frame', 'ObjectPool, ReelSymbol, FrameBuilder'],
      ['core, per reel', 'Reel, ReelMotion, ReelAxis, ReelViewport'],
      ['spin', 'phases, modes, SpinController'],
      ['core, the set', 'ReelSet, ReelSetBuilder'],
      ['spotlight, wins, board', 'presentation on top of the set'],
    ].map(([t, sub], i) => (
      <Box x={160} y={20 + i * 74} w={400} h={56} title={t} sub={sub} mono={false} kind={i === 4 ? 'primary' : 'subtle'} />
    ))}
    {[0, 1, 2, 3, 4].map((i) => (
      <Edge from={[360, 76 + i * 74]} to={[360, 94 + i * 74]} />
    ))}
  </Diagram>
  <Callout kind="remember">
    You construct one builder, you get one container, you speak three verbs.
    The reels are strips under a mask, the controller only does timing, and
    everything reports back through typed events. The rest of the tour zooms
    into each of those sentences.
  </Callout>

  <p>
    Read on in order, or jump to the page that answers your question. Every
    page opens with the idea in plain words, then shows it, then names the
    code.
  </p>
</Architecture>
